A F F I D A V I T

	I, Sumedha Thanda W/o Udhay Panjela, aged about 38 years, Occ: Pvt. Employee, permanent resident at 5 Fawley Lodge, 1 Millennium Drive, London, United Kingdom, E14 3GA. Currently in India residing at R/o H.No.10-1/1, Narsingi, Medak, Telangana 502 248. having temporarily come down to Hyderabad, do hereby solemnly affirm and state on oath as follows under:-

1.	I submit that, I am the deponent herein and petitioner in the above writ petition as such I am well acquainted with facts of the case. 

2.	The present Writ Petition is filed to challenge the arbitrary and illegal deboarding of the Minor Petitioner, Niketan Panjela, on 23-10-2025, and the subsequent travel restraint imposed by the Respondents for over 60 days. This restraint has been enforced without any written notice, in direct violation of the mandatory procedures under the Passports Act, 1967 and also the Indian Citizenship Act, 1955, which is illegal and arbitrary. Furthermore, the Respondents' insistence on the surrender of the Minor's passport and the imposition of overstay fines is contrary to his statutory status under Section 4(1A) of the Citizenship Act, 1955, violating his fundamental rights under Articles 14 and 21 of the Constitution of India. 

Since our initial formal outreach on October 24, 2025, we have made over a dozen representations to various authorities, including the Ministry of External Affairs and the Ministry of Home Affairs. Despite this, we have encountered persistent administrative silence for over 60 days. Following our application for an exit permit, officials at the FRRO Hyderabad verbally informed us that the permit would only be granted upon the surrender of my minor son’s Indian passport and the payment of a fine for an alleged overstay. Crucially, despite our repeated verbal requests and my formal email dated December 19, 2025, specifically requesting a written clarification of the legal basis for these demands, the authorities have expressly refused to provide any written communication or formal order justifying their position . In the absence of such a written order citing a specific statutory provision, these demands appear to be a direct attempt to override my son’s vested rights as a Citizen of India by Descent under Section 4 of the Citizenship Act, 1955.

3.	The petitioner submits that at the time of deboarding on October 23, 2025, the immigration officers orally relied upon an undated stamp appearing in the minor’s Indian passport. It is submitted that the said stamp is generic and administrative in nature, intended to apply in cases of voluntary acquisition of foreign nationality by the passport holder, and has no statutory application to a minor protected under Section 4(1A) of the Citizenship Act, 1955. No written order was issued to the petitioner or the minor on the basis of the said endorsement.

4.	I submit that I moved to the United Kingdom in September 2010 to pursue my Masters in Business Administration. Upon completion of my studies, I secured employment with Barclays in October 2012, where I have been continuously employed to date. I married Udhay Panjela, in November 2019. Subsequently, in September 2020, I obtained British Citizenship. Our son, the Minor Petitioner Niketan Panjela, was born on March 23, 2022. At the time of the Minor's birth, I was a British Citizen, whereas his father, Udhay Panjela, was an Indian Citizen. Consequently, the Minor became a citizen of India by descent under Section 4(1) of the Citizenship Act, 1955. His birth was thereafter duly registered with the Indian Mission on April 25, 2022, well within the statutory period of one year. It is further submitted that the Minor's Indian passport was applied for in the first instance in compliance with the Citizenship Act, and only subsequent to that was the British passport applied for. Verbal assertions by the local FRRO that my son must surrender his Indian passport are legally untenable, as the passport remains the primary statutory proof of his citizenship—distinct from mere identity documents like Aadhaar or PAN. Relevant extracts, section 4(1) from the Citizenship Act, 1955 is as below :-

“4. Citizenship by descent.―2[(1) A person born outside India shall be a citizen of India by descent,―
(a) on or after the 26th day of January, 1950, but before the 10th day of December, 1992, if his father is a citizen of India at the time of his birth; or
(b) on or after the 10th day of December, 1992, if either of his parents is a citizen of India at the time of his birth:

Provided further that if either of the parents of a person referred to in clause (b) was a citizen of India by descent only, that person shall not be a citizen of India by virtue of this section, unless―

(a) his birth is registered at an Indian consulate within one year of its occurrence or on or after the 10th day of December, 1992, whichever is later, or, with the permission of the Central Government,
after the expiry of the said period; or

(b) either of his parents is, at the time of his birth, in service under a Government in India:

Provided also that on or after the commencement of the Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2003 (6 of 2004), a person shall not be a citizen of India by virtue of this section, unless his birth is registered at an Indian consulate in such form and in such manner, as may be prescribed,―

(i) within one year of its occurrence or the commencement of the Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2003(6 of 2004), whichever is later; or

(ii) with the permission of the Central Government, after the expiry of the said period:

Provided also that no such birth shall be registered unless the parents of such person declare, in such form and in such manner as may be prescribed, that the minor does not hold the passport of another country.

(1A) A minor who is a citizen of India by virtue of this section and is also a citizen of any other country shall cease to be a citizen of India if he does not renounce the citizenship or nationality of another
country within six months of attaining full age.]”

5.	The petitioner submits that the present writ petition is confined to seeking protection of the minor’s liberty and facilitation of travel. The petitioner undertakes that she has no intention of violating Indian law and shall abide by any future statutory requirement, policy change, or final judicial determination etc, including compliance with any lawful requirement relating to surrender or renunciation, if and when the minor attains majority or if so directed by a competent court or authority.

6.	Independent Status: I further submit that whilst the father subsequently acquired foreign citizenship on 15-04-2025 — thereby triggering Section 9(1) of the Act —such voluntary acquisition has no legal bearing on the Minor’s status. As clarified by the Hon’ble Supreme Court in Union of India v. Pranav Srinivasan (2024 INSC 792), the legal status of a parent at the time of birth is the decisive factor for Indian citizenship. In that case, the Court rejected the claim for resumption of citizenship under Section 8(2) precisely because the parents had automatically ceased to be Indian citizens under Section 9(1) before the child was born. The Court held that since the parents’ citizenship ended by operation of law (Section 9) rather than voluntary renunciation (Section 8), the provision for the derivative loss of a minor's citizenship was never triggered, and there was no citizenship for the child to 'resume'.




Applying this logic to the present case, my son Niketan Panjela was born a Citizen of India by Descent under Section 4, as at least one parent was an Indian citizen at the time of his birth. His citizenship is a vested statutory right established at the time of birth that cannot be terminated derivatively just because his parents later acquired foreign nationality voluntarily. 

7.	I submit that the 60-day restraint has caused irreversible developmental harm to the minor and disrupted his education. As the sole earner on unpaid leave since 10-12-2025, I am losing approximately £987 pay weekly; this, combined with mounting legal and travel costs, poses an imminent risk to my employment stability in the UK and our family's economic survival. The Respondents’ actions force an illegal choice between family separation and economic ruin. For which the petitioners are at liberty to seek the appropriate steps as advised by the legal opinion for recovery of amount. 

9.	The petitioner, under the aforementioned facts, and circumstances, having left with no other efficacious alternative remedy is constrained to approach this Hon’ble Court to invoke its extraordinary jurisdiction under Article 226 of the Constitution of India. The delay, if any, in approaching this Hon’ble Court is solely attributable to the petitioner’s bona fide efforts to resolve the issue administratively, including repeated written representations to the Respondent authorities seeking clarification of law and a speaking order.

10.	The petitioner has not filed any other writ, suit or other legal proceedings for securing the relief sought for in this writ petition. 

WHEREFORE, it is most respectfully prayed that this Hon’ble Court may be pleased to issue a Writ of Mandamus or any other appropriate Writ, Order, or Direction:
To DECLARE the action of the Respondents in deboarding the Minor Petitioner, Niketan Panjela, on 23-10-2025 and their subsequent inaction in issuing an Exit Permit—while arbitrarily insisting on the surrender of his Indian Passport and payment of overstay fines—as illegal, arbitrary, and a violation of the Citizenship Act, 1955, and Articles 14 and 21 of the Constitution of India; 
CONSEQUENTIAL DIRECTION: Without prejudice to final adjudication on citizenship status to DIRECT the Respondents to issue a valid Exit Permit to the Minor Petitioner to allow him to travel to the United Kingdom without coercive conditions;
OTHER RELIEFS: To PASS such other relief or reliefs as this Hon’ble Court deems fit and proper in the interests of justice and equity.

